---
// firma2-hover-card — THE hover card. One object: what KIND of thing this is,
// WHICH one it is, and the few facts that place it — raised off whatever is
// slotted in as its trigger, on hover or on focus.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS: THERE WERE TWO OF THEM. The classification chips in Key facts
// and the people in Contacts and Comments are the same interaction — an entity
// in a data row, with more to say than the row has space for — and they had two
// implementations, one inside firma2-editable-field and one inside
// firma2-contact-card. That is the ordinary cost of duplication (two treatments
// drifting), plus a sharper one: THE GEOMETRY BELOW IS SUBTLE AND BOTH COPIES
// HAD IT WRONG, in the same two ways, and fixing one did nothing for the other.
// Measured on the project rail, 2026-08-24:
//
//   - CLIPPED. Two of the three classification cards on Red Clover Valley were
//     cut off at the rail's left edge (3px and 14px). The wide chips escaped and
//     the narrow ones did not, which is the worst possible failure mode: it
//     looks fine on the record you happen to open.
//   - UNREACHABLE. Both cards sat 8px below their trigger with the pointer over
//     nothing in between, so the card closed on the way to it unless you
//     approached at exactly the right angle.
//
// Both are consequences of how esa-popover positions, and both are fixed once,
// here. That is the argument for the component: not that the markup repeated,
// but that the CORRECTNESS repeated and only got applied once.
//
// THE TWO FIXES, and why they take the shape they do:
//
// 1. THE PANEL IS EXACTLY AS WIDE AS ITS TRIGGER ROW. esa-popover centres its
//    panel on its anchor (`left: 50%` and a translate) and has no start/end
//    alignment; the rail, meanwhile, is a scroll container, and `overflow-y:
//    auto` clips the x axis too — so anything leaving the column is cut, not
//    merely overhung. Centre a box on an anchor of its OWN width, though, and
//    the two left edges coincide: the card drops under the trigger flush with
//    it, like a dropdown, and cannot leave a column the trigger fits in. The
//    row is widened to `span` for the same reason. (`overflow-x: clip` with an
//    `overflow-clip-margin` would have let the panel spill out instead, and was
//    measured: `clip` computes to `hidden` when the other axis is a scroll
//    container, so the margin is ignored. It is not available.)
//
//    THE ARROW GOES WITH IT. It is drawn at the panel's centre, which is now
//    the middle of a wide row — it pointed at empty space and made the card read
//    as belonging to the column rather than to the thing under it. With the
//    edges aligned there is nothing left for it to point at.
//
// 2. THE GAP IS PADDING, NOT `offset`. esa-popover renders the panel INSIDE its
//    anchor, so travelling from the trigger onto the card does not close it —
//    but only while the pointer stays in the anchor's box, and the 8px `offset`
//    puts that air outside it. Zero offset, and the same 8px is rebuilt as
//    padding on the trigger row, which is inside: identical air, a bridge
//    rather than a moat.
//
// THE RIGHT FIX IS A HUB ONE and this is the interim. esa-popover needs
// `align="start" | "center" | "end"`; with it, both of the above collapse to one
// attribute and the trigger goes back to being only as wide as the thing it is.
// Worth a /request-lego — and when it lands, it lands for every card at once,
// which is the second reason this file exists.
//
// THE SHAPE IS FIXED, AND IT IS THE ONE THE CLASSIFICATION CARD ALREADY HAD:
// kind, then title, then lines. A reader who has learned one card in this app
// has learned all of them. The `data-card-*` hooks are load-bearing — they are
// how firma2-editable-field repaints a member's card at runtime after an edit,
// by cloning this markup and writing into those three nodes — so they are part
// of the contract, not decoration.
//
// A LINE CAN BE COPYABLE, and that lives here rather than in the caller for the
// same reason everything else does. A card is often the only place a fact is
// written down — an address, a reference number — and selecting text inside a
// box that closes when the pointer leaves it is close to impossible, so the
// affordance has to be a control. It docks to the END OF ITS OWN LINE: the
// button copies THAT value, so putting it in a card-level corner would leave
// the reader working out what it acts on (it was built that way first, and read
// exactly that wrong). The card owns the clipboard call and the receipt because
// they are the same in every card; the caller owns only which line and what to
// call the button.
//
// A CONTROL INSIDE A HOVER PANEL IS REACHABLE HERE, which is not generally true
// and is worth stating: the pointer travels the bridge into the card without
// leaving the anchor, and Tab lands on the button straight after the trigger
// because esa-popover ignores focus moving between the two. The same button
// docked to the RAIL ROW instead would be a second tab stop on every row for an
// act that only makes sense once you have read the card.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: esa-popover IS the lego and this composes it — the panel
// surface, the placement, the open/close wiring, the Escape handler, the
// focusin/focusout keyboard parity and the aria-expanded on the slotted trigger
// are all its. Walked the catalog (`ls node_modules/@esa/ecology/src/
// components/`) for a hover/entity/preview card: esa-card is a page-section
// surface, esa-tooltip is a string of text with no structure, esa-popover is
// exactly the primitive underneath this. Beacon is not cloned on this machine.
// What is bespoke is the two geometry rules above, and they exist only because
// the lego cannot yet express them.
import EsaButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-button.astro';

/**
 * A fact the reader is likely to want OUT of the card — an address, an
 * identifier. `copy` is the button's accessible name and its hover tooltip, so
 * it says what lands on the clipboard ("Copy email address"), and its presence
 * is what turns the control on. Nothing else about the line changes.
 */
export interface HoverCardCopyLine {
  text: string;
  copy: string;
}

interface Props {
  /** What KIND of thing this is. "Portfolio goal", "Grant manager". */
  kind: string;
  /** WHICH one — normally the same string the trigger shows. */
  title: string;
  /**
   * The facts that place it. One short line each; keep it to three or four. A
   * plain string is a line to read; a HoverCardCopyLine is a line to read AND
   * take away.
   */
  lines?: (string | HoverCardCopyLine)[];
  /**
   * THE WIDTH OF THE COLUMN THIS CARD LIVES IN — the trigger row stretches to
   * it and the panel is sized from it, so the card lands flush and unclipped.
   * See fix 1 above; this is the one number a caller has to know.
   *
   * Default is the record rail's content measure: a 25rem track less the two
   * --spacing-500 steps the rail spends on padding, to the nearest rung. A
   * caller inside a box with its own inset passes a smaller value.
   */
  span?: string;
}

const { kind, title, lines = [], span = '21rem' } = Astro.props;
---
<esa-popover
  class="firma2-hover-card__pop"
  trigger="hover"
  position="bottom"
  has-arrow="false"
  offset="0"
  style={`--_span: ${span}`}
>
  <span class="firma2-hover-card__row repel" data-gap="xs">
    <!-- THE TRIGGER. Whatever the caller puts here: a chip inside a link, a
         person's name as a mailto. It must be FOCUSABLE, or the card is
         pointer-only — esa-popover opens on focusin, but only if something in
         here can take focus. -->
    <slot />
    <!-- Anything else the row carries — the Comments thread puts the date here.
         It rides inside the trigger because the trigger has to span the row
         (fix 1) and two things cannot both span it; hovering it therefore
         raises the card too, which is right, since the row is the thing. -->
    <slot name="trailing" />
  </span>

  <div slot="content" class="firma2-hover-card stack" data-gap="2xs">
    <span class="firma2-hover-card__kind typography-label-sm" data-card-kind>{kind}</span>
    <span class="typography-title-sm-strong" data-card-title>{title}</span>
    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-card-lines>
      {lines.map((line) => (
        typeof line === 'string' ? (
          <span class="firma2-hover-card__line typography-body-sm">{line}</span>
        ) : (
          <!-- A COPYABLE LINE. The text keeps the plain line's treatment
               exactly — a fact does not change how it reads because it gained
               an affordance — and the control docks to the END OF ITS OWN LINE,
               which is the whole point: the button copies THIS value, not the
               card, so it has to be adjacent to the value or it is asking the
               reader to work out what it acts on. -->
          <!-- A CLUSTER, NOT A REPEL, and the difference is the whole point of
               putting the control on the line at all. `.repel` pushes its
               groups to opposite edges, which aims the button at the PANEL and
               leaves a run of empty space between it and the address — read as
               a card-level control that happens to be on this row. Clustered,
               it sits immediately after the value, and what it copies is the
               thing it is touching. -->
          <span class="firma2-hover-card__line firma2-hover-card__line--copy cluster" data-gap="2xs">
            <span class="firma2-hover-card__line-text typography-body-sm">{line.text}</span>
            <!-- `xs` — the in-row rung, the same one firma2-editable-field
                 gives the ↗ it docks in a field. NOT the spoke's `md` control
                 rung: that rule governs FORM CONTROLS, where the hub's small
                 steps put field text under the 13px readability floor and
                 AppLayout pins md boxes to 40px. An icon-only chrome button has
                 no text to fall below a floor, is not in the pinned set, and at
                 any larger step it outweighs the line it belongs to — which is
                 exactly what it looked like at `sm`. -->
            <EsaButton
              variant="chrome"
              iconOnly
              icon="copy"
              size="xs"
              label={line.copy}
              data-copy-value={line.text}
            />
            <!-- THE RECEIPT, doing both jobs from one node: the word a sighted
                 reader sees and the announcement a screen reader hears. Empty
                 at rest, so it costs no space until it has something to say.
                 AFTER the button, which is what the cluster forces and what is
                 right anyway: docked to the value, the button has the line's
                 slack on its right, so a receipt that filled from the left
                 would shove the control out from under the pointer that just
                 pressed it.

                 a11y-checked: empty in the SOURCE, not permanently — the
                 delegated handler in this file's script writes "Copied" (or
                 "Copy failed") into it on every copy and clears it 2s later,
                 which is the announcement, and a live region has to be present
                 and empty BEFORE the change for that change to be announced at
                 all. Verified in a browser: pressing the button sets
                 textContent and the receipt is read. A shared page-level
                 announcer was the alternative and is worse here — it would
                 split the word the reader sees from the word they hear, and
                 this element is deliberately both. -->
            <span class="firma2-hover-card__receipt typography-label-xs" role="status" data-copy-receipt></span>
          </span>
        )
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</esa-popover>

<style>
  .firma2-hover-card__pop {
    /* Its own line in a stack, and a flex item that does not shrink below its
       row in a `.repel` — either way the anchor gets the width fix 1 needs. */
    display: block;
  }

  .firma2-hover-card__row {
    /* AN EXPLICIT LENGTH, which is the part that cannot be avoided.
       esa-popover's anchor is `display: inline-block` inside its own shadow root
       — unreachable from here — so it shrink-wraps whatever is slotted into it,
       and a `100%` on this row would resolve against that shrink-to-fit width,
       i.e. against itself. Only a real length widens it. */
    min-inline-size: var(--_span);

    /* THE BRIDGE — fix 2. This is the air between the trigger and the card, and
       it is padding rather than the popover's `offset` because padding is
       inside the anchor's box: a pointer crossing it has not left the trigger. */
    padding-block-end: var(--spacing-200, 0.5rem);
  }

  .firma2-hover-card {
    /* THE PANEL MATCHES THE ROW — fix 1. The arithmetic runs backwards from the
       panel to its content, because the panel sizes to what is in it: the row's
       width, less the panel's own padding and border, both quoted from the
       tokens esa-popover uses for them (`--_popover-padding` is --spacing-300;
       the border is --border-width-default). Re-derive if the lego re-points
       either. */
    inline-size: calc(
      var(--_span) - 2 * var(--spacing-300, 0.75rem) - 2 *
        var(--border-width-default, 1px)
    );
  }

  .firma2-hover-card__kind {
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #646464);
  }

  /* A line can be an email address — one unbreakable token in a card narrower
     than the column. Harmless on prose. */
  .firma2-hover-card__line,
  .firma2-hover-card__line-text {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* THE BUTTON SETS THIS ROW'S HEIGHT — it is a 32px control on a 17px line, and
     the negative block margin is what stops that from opening a gap the other
     lines do not have. The value is half the difference between the xs button
     (a 14px icon, two --spacing-200 steps and a border) and the body-sm leading
     it sits on, so the TEXT keeps the card's rhythm and the control overhangs
     it symmetrically. Re-measure if either rung moves. */
  .firma2-hover-card__line--copy {
    margin-block: calc(var(--spacing-100, 0.25rem) * -1);
  }

  /* The control never shrinks — a long address wraps INSIDE its own item and
     the button stays a 32px square docked to the end of it. */
  .firma2-hover-card__line--copy > :not(.firma2-hover-card__line-text) {
    flex: none;
  }

  /* SECONDARY INK, NOT SUCCESS GREEN. "Copied" is a receipt for something the
     reader just did on purpose, not an outcome they were waiting to learn — a
     green flash on the smallest event on the screen would make it the loudest.
     The failure case takes the danger token, because that one IS news. */
  .firma2-hover-card__receipt {
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #646464);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .firma2-hover-card__receipt[data-copy-failed] {
    color: var(--color-content-utility-danger, #641723);
  }
</style>

<script>
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-popover';

  // COPY A LINE OUT OF A CARD — every copyable line on the page, wired once.
  //
  // ONE DELEGATED LISTENER, not one per card. A project record renders a card
  // per contact in the Contacts tab and one per comment in the thread, and
  // Astro bundles this module once however many times the component appears —
  // so the handler has to be written for "any copy button, ever", which
  // delegation is and a per-node loop is not. It also covers the cards
  // firma2-editable-field repaints at runtime, which no loop at load could.
  //
  // navigator.clipboard NEEDS A SECURE CONTEXT (https, or localhost). Both the
  // deployed site and `astro preview` qualify; a page opened over plain http on
  // a LAN address would not, and that is what the catch is for — the value
  // stays on screen, so the fallback is the reader's own selection rather than
  // a control that pretends to have worked.
  const CLEAR_AFTER_MS = 2000;

  // Per-button, so two cards copied in quick succession do not clear each
  // other's receipt. WeakMap rather than a dataset field: the timer is runtime
  // state, not markup, and it goes when the node does.
  const timers = new WeakMap<Element, number>();

  function report(button: Element, message: string, failed: boolean): void {
    const receipt = button
      .closest('.firma2-hover-card__line--copy')
      ?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-copy-receipt]');
    if (!receipt) return;

    receipt.textContent = message;
    receipt.toggleAttribute('data-copy-failed', failed);

    const pending = timers.get(button);
    if (pending) window.clearTimeout(pending);
    timers.set(
      button,
      window.setTimeout(() => {
        receipt.textContent = '';
        receipt.removeAttribute('data-copy-failed');
        timers.delete(button);
      }, CLEAR_AFTER_MS),
    );
  }

  document.addEventListener('click', async (event) => {
    // `closest` from the event target, because the click lands on the <svg>
    // inside esa-button's native <button> — the element carrying the value is
    // an ancestor of whatever was actually hit.
    const button = (event.target as Element | null)?.closest?.('[data-copy-value]');
    if (!button) return;

    const value = (button as HTMLElement).dataset.copyValue ?? '';
    if (!value) return;

    try {
      await navigator.clipboard.writeText(value);
      report(button, 'Copied', false);
    } catch {
      report(button, 'Copy failed', true);
    }
  });
</script>
